    <div>
      <p><strong>The Tavis Home</strong></p>
      <p><strong>250 4th Ave NW</strong></p>

      <p>
        There is some mystery as to the actual year this home was built. It is
        believed that it was built in 1903 by a Mr. Porter and that it is one of
        the oldest homes in Mandan today. The Herman J. Tavis family moved into
        the home in 1905.
      </p>

      <p>
        Herman J. Tavis was born in 1865 in West Virginia. His parents came from
        Germany in 1857 and settled in West Virginia as farmers. In 1885,
        Herman, along with his parents and younger brother, came to Dakota
        Territory and settled near Glen Ullin. Herman married Elizabeth Hess in
        1892. The couple left Glen Ullin in 1905 and moved to Mandan, where they
        made their home at this location.
      </p>

      <p>
        Herman was in the real estate business and also owned and operated a
        small garage where he sold gasoline and performed minor repair work on
        cars. He was a member of the Mandan City Band and was noted for his old
        time fiddling, having won high places in several contests.
      </p>

      <p>
        Herman continued in real estate until he retired in 1926. At that time
        he sold the home to his nephew, Edward L. Tavis, and he and Elizabeth
        moved to Englewood, California. Although Herman retired, he wanted
        something to keep himself busy, so he opened a small popcorn stand. It
        was far more successful than he thought it would be.
      </p>

      <p>
        Herman and Elizabeth were married almost 50 years. Elizabeth died in
        the early 1930s and Herman in the early 1940s.
      </p>

      <p>
        Edward, Herman’s nephew, owned the home until 1928. It was then sold to
        Mr. and Mrs. Peter Wagner, who owned the home until 1932. The home was
        then purchased by Mr. Frank Wagner. The home is currently owned by
        Daniel Meidinger, who purchased it in 2009. The home appears much as it
        did when the Tavis family owned the home.
      </p>

      <p class="source-credit">
        Draft transcription from the photographed sign. The printed address is
        preserved here as source wording; it is not the property’s correct
        address.
      </p>
